---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import TeamGrid from '../components/TeamGrid.astro';
import CredentialList from '../components/CredentialList.astro';
import { BRAND, CONTACT, SITE } from '../config';
import { graph, organizationNode, websiteNode, webPageNode, breadcrumbNode } from '../lib/schema';

const path = '/about/';
const title = `About ${BRAND.name} | Nashville Septic Service`;
const description = 'What we do, the standards the work follows, where we work and how we price it. No founding story, because the work is the point.';
const trail = [{ name: 'Home', href: '/' }, { name: 'About', href: path }];
const schema = graph([organizationNode(), websiteNode(), webPageNode(path, title, description, SITE.lastReviewed), breadcrumbNode(path, trail)]);
/* Written about the work, not a backstory. No founding year, no invented owner,
   no team photos, no "since 20XX". Every sentence here is true. */
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} schema={schema}>
  <section class="container-page pt-6 pb-10 border-b border-hairline">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
    <h1 class="mt-6 text-[2rem] md:text-5xl leading-[1.13]">About {BRAND.name}</h1>
  </section>

  <section class="container-page py-14">
    <article class="prose-article">
      <p>
        <strong>{BRAND.name}</strong> is a septic tank pumping company serving {CONTACT.serviceAreaLong}.
        It pumps, cleans and inspects residential and commercial septic systems, services grease traps
        and interceptors, and diagnoses drain fields that have stopped accepting.
      </p>

      <h2>What we actually do on a visit</h2>
      <p>
        Both tank lids are located and opened — hand-dug where they are buried, which on wooded and
        large-lot Davidson County properties is most of the time. Sludge and scum depths are measured
        against the tank's capacity before anything is pumped, because that measurement is what tells
        you whether your interval is right. The tank is then pumped completely: liquid, floating scum
        and settled sludge, followed by a backflush. A liquid-only draw-down leaves behind exactly the
        material that threatens your drain field, and makes a tank look serviced while the problem
        stays where it was.
      </p>
      <p>
        With the tank empty, the inlet baffle, the outlet baffle and the effluent filter are inspected
        — the three components that fail quietly and take a drain field with them. You get told what
        was found, not just that the tank was emptied.
      </p>

      <h2>The standards this work follows</h2>
      <p>
        Septic tank pumping contractors in Tennessee are governed by Tenn. Comp. R. &amp; Regs.
        0400-48-01-.20. Subsurface sewage disposal systems fall under TDEC's Subsurface Sewage
        Disposal Program, and Davidson County administers its own septic program as a TDEC contract
        county, with Metro Public Health's Environmental Engineering Services handling soils
        interpretation, system design, percolation-test monitoring and groundwater protection.
      </p>
      <p>
        Grease work follows Metro Water Services' FOG program: traps serviced at least every 30 days,
        interceptors at least every 90, service whenever fats, oils, grease and settled solids reach
        25% of the unit's capacity, certified hauler manifests, and three-year record retention.
      </p>

      <h2>Where we work, and why it is that shape</h2>
      <p>
        {CONTACT.serviceArea}. Septic in this county is concentrated in the areas the postwar sewer
        extension never reached — a project that began soon after the 1963 consolidation and ran into
        the early 1990s — plus the large-lot areas shaped by the county's 1946 decision to require
        half-acre minimum lots, and larger where soils were poor, expressly to give drain fields room.
      </p>
      <p>
        We build a service-area page only where there is something real to say about septic there. If
        an area came onto mains sewer, we do not claim to serve its septic systems, because it does
        not have any.
      </p>

      <h2>How we price it</h2>
      <p>
        Ranges are published on this site and there is a
        <a href="/tools/septic-pumping-cost-estimator/">cost estimator</a> that gives you a number from
        three questions without an email address or a phone call. You get a band before we come out
        and a firm figure before we do anything that changes it. If the tank is opened and something
        needs doing that was not in the quote — compacted sludge, a corroded baffle, a split effluent
        filter — you are told what it costs and you decide.
      </p>

      <h2>What you will not find on this site</h2>
      <p>
        There are no reviews, star ratings, testimonials or customer counts here, and no claims about
        years in business or jobs completed. That is deliberate. This is a new operation, none of
        those things exist yet, and inventing them would be both dishonest and, under the FTC's rules
        on fabricated reviews, unlawful.
      </p>
      <p>
        What stands in their place is everything that is true on day one: published pricing, the
        actual regulations the work follows, the specific local geology and history that shape septic
        in this county, a calculator that answers your first question without a sales call, and a
        straight comparison of what a cheap pump-out leaves behind. When real reviews exist, they will
        appear here — and not one word before.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you have used us and are willing to say so, the <a href="/leave-a-review/">review page</a>
        explains how.
      </p>
    </article>
    <div class="mt-10 max-w-md"><CredentialList /></div>
  </section>

  <TeamGrid />
  <CtaBand heading="Questions about your system?" body={`Call ${CONTACT.phoneDisplay}. ${CONTACT.hoursHuman}`} ctaLabel="Get a price" />
</BaseLayout>
